            <ArticleTitle>The self-reported health status of prisoners in New South Wales.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>344-50</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To describe the physical health of the New South Wales prisoner population.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Cross-sectional random sample of adult men and women prisoners.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">29 New South Wales correctional centres (27 male and two female).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">747 men and 167 women.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Despite the comparatively young population, 81% of women and 65% of men had at least one chronic health condition; 41% of men and 59% of women reported multiple health problems. The most common conditions were back problems, poor eyesight, arthritis, high blood pressure and asthma. Chronic conditions were more prevalent among women prisoners. Thirty-seven per cent of women and 28% of men rated their health as either 'poor' or 'fair' compared with 16% of women and 15% of men in the general NSW community. Psychiatric medication was more commonly prescribed to women than men (25% vs. 13%; p &lt; 0.001). Similarly, methadone maintenance was more common among women than men (39% vs. 13%; p &lt; 0.001).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Men and women prisoners in NSW have multiple chronic health conditions. While not desirable, incarceration presents an opportunity to initiate treatment to improve the health of this disadvantaged group.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
